162    /** FUNCTION otap_schema.has_constraint
163    * Checks if a given table has a constraint of the given type. Optional you can specify column and constraint name.
164    * Supported constraint types:
165    * C - Check constraint on a table
166    * P - Primary key
167    * U - Unique key
168    * R - Referential integrity - use has_ref_constraint for more options
169    * V - With check option, on a view
170    * O - With read only, on a view
171    * H - Hash expression
172    * F - Constraint that involves a REF column - use has_ref_constraint for more options
173    * S is not supported, could not find or create an example to examine. SUPPLEMENTAL LOG clause only reflects in CDEF$ and CCOL$.
174    *
175    * @param p_table_name Mandatory. The table to be checked for constraint, take as is. Case sensitive. Missing value will cause test to fail.
176    * @param o_error Error information, if any, on the test executed.
177    * @param p_constraint_type Mandatory. A valid constraint type. Not case sensitive. Wrong values will cause the test to fail.
178    * @param p_column_name Optional. The column that is part of the constraint. Case sensitive.
179    * @param p_constraint Optional. The name of the constraint. Case sensitive.
180    * @param p_schema The schema to use. If NULL current schema is used. Case sensitive.
181    * @param p_expected_result The expected test result as number. Default is test passed. See otap_constants.
182    *
183    * @return The test result as number, either otap_constants.OTAP_NUM_TEST_PASSED, otap_constants.OTAP_NUM_TEST_FAILED or otap_constants.OTAP_NUM_TEST_UNDEFINED.
