797    /** FUNCTION otap_test.match_regex
798    * Checks given data of type VARCHAR2 against an Oracle REGEX expression. Uses REGEXP_LIKE.
799    * ATTENTION Oracle REGEX implementation is not standard. Unix regex which work like charm take hours to implement in
800    * Oracle REGEX to work as desired. Test your expression well with Oracle before using it.
801    *
802    * Easiest way to check is SELECT COUNT(*) FROM dual WHERE regexp_like('your string', 'your regex', 'regex param');
803    * Should result in 1 if successful checked. You may want to prepare a with block with different string to pass them
804    * through the regular expression.
805    *
806    * @param p_have The data to check.
807    * @param p_regex A valid Oracle regular expression that p_have must match.
808    * @param p_description The test description if any. If not given, a description is generated, see template.
809    * @param p_param Parameter for REGEXP_LIKE. 'i' is case insensitive. See Oracle documentation for details, https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/21/sqlrf/Pattern-matching-Conditions.html#GUID-D2124F3A-C6E4-4CCA-A40E-2FFCABFD8E19.
810    * @param p_expected_result The expected test result as number. Default is test passed. See otap_constants.
811    * @param p_schema A schema override of the current test session if needed, taken as is. Compares are not clearly associated to a schema. Case sensitive.
812    *
813    * @return The test result as text.
814    */
815    FUNCTION match_regex( p_have	       IN VARCHAR2
816  			   , p_regex	       IN VARCHAR2
817  			   , p_description     IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT NULL
818  			   , p_param	       IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT NULL
819  			   , p_expected_result IN NUMBER   DEFAULT otap_constants.OTAP_NUM_TEST_PASSED
820  			   , p_schema	       IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT SYS_CONTEXT('USERENV', 'CURRENT_SCHEMA')
821  			   )
822  	 RETURN VARCHAR2
823    ;
